<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cielito Lindo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">popularized <a href="/years/1882">1882</a>; its first verse from a Spanish song of the early 17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Cielito Lindo&quot; (roughly &quot;Lovely Sweet One&quot;) is a Mexican folk song, or copla, popularized in 1882 by the Mexican author Quirino Mendoza y Cortés (c. 1862–1957). It is widely known by words from its refrain, &quot;Canta y no llores&quot;, or as the &quot;Ay, Ay, Ay, Ay song&quot;. Its verses follow the seguidilla, a classical Castilian stanza of seven lines of alternating seven and five syllables, and singers add and drop verses freely.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In an article titled ¡Hasta que me cayó el veinte!, the researcher Ortega traced the first verse to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the early 17th century, when armed bandits took refuge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a> and travellers feared for their lives crossing the range. The words appeared in a song of that time that hinted at this fear. Over time the verse was romanticised, so that the face of the beloved became the Sierra Morena and her eyes the thieves who lived there. The verse was set to other melodies before Quirino Mendoza adapted it to his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Some debate whether the words refer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> or to the Sierra de Morones in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>. Most Mexicans, however, read the line as la Sierra, Morena, morena being a common term of endearment, so that the singer addresses the woman directly rather than naming a place. The song should not be confused with Cielito lindo huasteco, a Son Huasteco or Huapango from La Huasteca with quite different music, though its stanzas share the metre and the fillers cielito lindo and ay ay ay ay. Mariachis sometimes combine the two.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Mariachi bands commonly play the song, and it has been recorded in Spanish, English and other languages. A recording by the Mexican Tipica Orchestra for Victor is dated 26 November 1926, and Carl Sandburg printed it with a piano arrangement in The American Songbag of 1927. Pedro Infante sang it in the 1947 film Los tres García, Carmen Costa released a Portuguese version in 1942, and José Feliciano's version of 1989 won the Grammy Award for Best Latin Pop Performance. An adaptation was used in the finale of Shostakovich's 6th Symphony.</p><p>The tune travelled far from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. In Nazi-occupied Poland street musicians and the resistance sang it as the chant Teraz jest wojna, Now there is war. An advertisement for Fritos in which the Frito Bandito character sang a version with new words was considered by many Mexican nationals a racist insult to their culture. The song is widely sung by Mexicans abroad and at international events such as the Olympic Games and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">FIFA World Cup</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cielito Lindo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
